              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "That whitish, cloudy film on your water glass is highly common, and even though you are only drinking filtered water, it is actually a combination of three different things: **mineral deposits**, **biofilm**, and **saliva residue**. \n\nThe three culprits behind this film include:\n\n### 1. Mineral Deposits (Limescale)\nEven though your water comes from a refrigerator filter, standard fridge filters (which typically use activated carbon) only remove physical sediment, chlorine, and odors. They **do not** remove dissolved minerals like calcium and magnesium. \n* **How it forms:** Every time you take a sip or let the glass sit, tiny droplets of water cling to the sides. As that water evaporates into the air, the pure H\u2082O disappears, but the dissolved minerals stay behind. Over the course of a week, this evaporation happens hundreds of times, leaving behind a thin, chalky white layer of calcium carbonate (limescale).\n\n### 2. Biofilm (Microbial Growth)\nIf you reuse the same glass for a week without washing it, you are giving bacteria and fungal spores a perfect place to multiply. \n* **How it forms:** Bacteria from your mouth, hands, and the surrounding air land in the glass. In a moist environment, these microorganisms anchor themselves to the glass and secrete a slimy, protective sugary-protein matrix to live in. This is called a **biofilm**. On glass, a thin biofilm looks like a cloudy, translucent, or whitish-gray film that can feel slightly slick or greasy when wet.\n\n### 3. Saliva Proteins\nEvery time your lips touch the rim of the glass, a small amount of saliva is transferred.\n* **How it forms:** Human saliva is full of organic proteins, enzymes, and mucus. When these dry onto the glass, they leave behind a stubborn, cloudy residue that bonds tightly to the surface. \n\n---\n\n### How to Clean It Easily\nBecause of the minerals and biofilm, sometimes a quick rinse or regular dish soap isn't enough to make the glass look crystal clear. \n* **The Vinegar Solution:** Fill the glass with warm water and a splash of **white vinegar** (or rub it with a lemon) and let it sit for 10\u201315 minutes. The acid in the vinegar will chemically dissolve the alkaline calcium deposits and break up the organic proteins, allowing you to wipe the film away effortlessly.\n* **Baking Soda:** If there are stubborn spots, make a paste of baking soda and water and gently scrub with a sponge.\n\n### A Health Tip\nWhile the mineral buildup is completely harmless, drinking out of a glass that has developed a biofilm means you are reintroducing rapidly multiplying bacteria into your mouth. To keep things sanitary, it is highly recommended to **wash your water glass daily** rather than letting it go a full week!",
